Choice with case_sensitive=False returns the user's spelling instead of the declared choice

When a `click.Choice` is declared with `case_sensitive=False`, a value that differs only in case is accepted, but the callback receives the value exactly as the user typed it rather than the canonical choice string.

```python
@click.command()
@click.option('--mode', type=click.Choice(['Fast', 'Slow'], case_sensitive=False))
def cli(mode):
    click.echo(mode)
```

`cli --mode fast` prints `fast`; I expected `Fast`, matching the declared choice, so downstream comparisons against the declared list keep working.
